8
00:01:15.943 --> 00:01:17.711
-Hmm.
-[shutter clicking]

9
00:01:17.812 --> 00:01:20.647
Good morning. Morgan. [sighs]

10
00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.552
So. what do you think happened?

11
00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:29.056
Well. I think
we can rule out nothing.

12
00:01:29.156 --> 00:01:30.490
And we're off.

13
00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:34.061
Melted candles. empty glass.
clothes thrown everywhere.

14
00:01:34.161 --> 00:01:35.963
It looks like a wild night
that went south.

15
00:01:36.063 --> 00:01:37.832
[clicks tongue]
But was it murder?

16
00:01:37.932 --> 00:01:39.599
A hundred percent.
What. you don't think so?

17
00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:41.401
I think it was murder
and a cover-up.

18
00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:42.636
but I wanna know how you know.

19
00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:45.106
Sounds like a challenge.

20
00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.674
How fun. See these hand towels?

21
00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:51.078
You may remember that I used to
be a professional cleaner.

22
00:01:51.178 --> 00:01:52.146
-Hmm. Vaguely. yeah.
-Yes.

23
00:01:53.247 --> 00:01:54.748
The way that these towels
are folded are a disgrace.

24
00:01:54.849 --> 00:01:56.483
Look. the bottom two
have two edges.

25
00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:57.919
the top one has three edges.

26
00:01:59.387 --> 00:02:01.355
That means the killer
used this top towel
to clean up after himself.

27
00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:02.990
-cover his tracks.
-Hmm.

28
00:02:04.158 --> 00:02:07.128
Now. get down here
and see what I'm seeing.

29
00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:10.497
I can see just fine
from up here. thanks.
But what do you see?

30
00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:11.999
You see these streaks?

31
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:16.837
Any self-respecting housekeeper
is gonna use a microfiber mop
on a bathroom floor.

32
00:02:16.938 --> 00:02:20.107
Our murderer used that towel
to wipe up this area.

33
00:02:20.207 --> 00:02:21.541
Which suggests a cover-up.

34
00:02:22.977 --> 00:02:25.445
Exactly. Now. our vic...
Is it okay if I call him vic?
It's happening.

35
00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:26.713
Our vic was not alone.

36
00:02:26.814 --> 00:02:30.450
because no man's gonna spend
money on a hotel room this fancy

37
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.119
unless he's planning
on entertaining.

38
00:02:32.219 --> 00:02:35.189
And my guess: a sexy brunette.

39
00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:37.825
["Kaleidoscope" plays]

40
00:02:37.925 --> 00:02:39.626
Why? Did you find a hair sample?

41
00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:42.696
Nah. but blonde seems
too obvious. so I'm going with
brunette for now.

42
00:02:42.796 --> 00:02:45.465
Either way. she had to be
pretty attractive because--

43
00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:46.700
I don't mean
to be disrespectful.

44
00:02:46.801 --> 00:02:49.036
but the dead guy in the tub
is super hot.

45
00:02:49.136 --> 00:02:52.039
Morgan. please don't
ever comment on the hotness
of a victim ever again.

46
00:02:52.139 --> 00:02:55.675
Okay. The manager opened
the safe for us. and we found
the victim's wallet.

47
00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:58.145
Inside: $80 cash and an ID.

48
00:02:58.245 --> 00:03:00.647
Ernest Lozano. Mexico City.

49
00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:01.816
Do a deep dive.

50
00:03:01.916 --> 00:03:03.851
Anything. everything
you can find on the guy.

51
00:03:03.951 --> 00:03:06.553
Stop by the security center
downstairs.
Look at last night's footage.

52
00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:07.654
-Got it.
-Yep.

53
00:03:09.023 --> 00:03:10.992
-Wonder what
he was drugged with.
-How do you know he was drugged?

54
00:03:11.092 --> 00:03:14.527
Well. 'cause who does
their dishes in a hotel room?
Except you. maybe.

55
00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.998
Anyway. this glass was
rinsed out and wiped clean.

56
00:03:17.098 --> 00:03:19.834
Why do that unless
you were trying to
get rid of white residue?

57
00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:22.569
-Notice anything else
about the glass?
-That's a whiskey tumbler.

58
00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:26.007
Lead-free. crystal-cut.
which is harder
and more durable than glass.

59
00:03:26.107 --> 00:03:29.542
Withstands most scratches.
Also refracts light beautifully.

60
00:03:29.944 --> 00:03:32.213
You want me to know
you drink whiskey so bad.

61
00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:34.581
Point being this piece
of glassware

62
00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:36.217
is far more expensive
than the others.

63
00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:39.020
It must have been
brought up to the room.

64
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.522
Lozano and his sexy brunette
stopped at the hotel bar
before coming to the room.

65
00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:44.125
-But you already knew that.
-Mm-hmm.

66
00:03:44.225 --> 00:03:47.028
The tumbler was rinsed out.
leaving little to zero
trace of whiskey.

67
00:03:47.128 --> 00:03:48.929
which strongly suggests
a cover-up.

68
00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.532
And why cover it up
unless it was murder?
Now. let's go hit the hotel bar.

69
00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:54.701
[bartender] I recognize him.

70
00:03:54.802 --> 00:03:57.570
He had a drink at the start
of my shift around 7:00 p.m.

71
00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:58.638
Was he with anyone?

72
00:03:59.206 --> 00:04:00.941
A sexy brunette. maybe?

73
00:04:01.042 --> 00:04:03.077
I'd say medium sexy.

74
00:04:03.177 --> 00:04:05.146
-Also. she was a blonde.
-Really?

75
00:04:06.047 --> 00:04:06.914
[brunette screams]

76
00:04:08.115 --> 00:04:10.217
-Did it seem like
business or pleasure?
-Maybe both.

77
00:04:10.317 --> 00:04:12.053
The guy was definitely
tying one on.

78
00:04:12.153 --> 00:04:14.554
Made sure to leave
a generous tip
before they bounced.

79
00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:15.990
Catch the name of his guest
by any chance?

80
00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:18.558
We were slammed. so I couldn't
really hear their convo.

81
00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:22.897
But I wanna say that her name
sounded like. uh. Candice
or Gladys or Janice.

82
00:04:22.997 --> 00:04:23.964
[phone chimes]

83
00:04:27.134 --> 00:04:28.735
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

84
00:04:28.836 --> 00:04:32.605
So. we scrubbed through
last night's footage.
and we found this at 8:02 p.m.

85
00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.576
Lozano and his mystery woman
got on the fourth floor.
into the room.

86
00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:40.081
And then at 8:33 p.m..
right there. she leaves.

87
00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.184
No one else used a key card
to enter the suite
until this morning

88
00:04:43.284 --> 00:04:45.286
when the cleaners discovered
Ernest Lozano's body.

89
00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:46.954
That must be our killer.

90
00:04:47.054 --> 00:04:49.256
I showed a photo
of our mystery woman
to the hotel doorman.

91
00:04:49.356 --> 00:04:54.128
He said she parked her red Kia
out front in a no-parking zone
around 7:00 p.m.

92
00:04:54.228 --> 00:04:56.931
Shortly after. LADOT arrived
and started writing tickets.

93
00:04:57.031 --> 00:04:58.933
So call LADOT.
see if we can ID the red car.

94
00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:00.101
I already called.

95
00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.304
They didn't issue a ticket
to a red Kia out front
of this hotel last night.

96
00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:06.340
Well. the only cars
that don't get ticketed
belong to cops and doctors.

97
00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:09.877
-Cops aren't issued red cars.
-Right. So it must be
vehicle code 21058.

98
00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:11.644
Mm-hmm.

99
00:05:12.913 --> 00:05:17.151
Yeah. it must be. Yeah.
Remind Oz about
vehicle code 21058.

100
00:05:17.251 --> 00:05:19.353
It's an emblem allowing
physicians responding
to emergencies

101
00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:20.955
to be exempt from
laws governing parking.

102
00:05:21.055 --> 00:05:22.689
There's a hospital
less than a mile from here.

103
00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:25.658
Swing by. see if anyone
recognizes the woman
from our hotel security footage.

104
00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:27.128
and we'll circle back
at the precinct.

105
00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:33.134
That sounds like
a city council issue.
not a major crimes issue.

106
00:05:34.001 --> 00:05:36.337
Of course. Keep me posted. Bye.

107
00:05:37.171 --> 00:05:38.671
How's the hotel case lookin'?

108
00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:41.809
Daphne and Oz ID'd our suspect
as a physician who works
in the neighborhood.

109
00:05:41.909 --> 00:05:43.643
They're bringing her in
for questioning as we speak.

110
00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:45.212
-You like her for it?
-Oh. very much.

111
00:05:46.814 --> 00:05:48.382
We have her on hotel security
footage entering and exiting
the victim's room

112
00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:49.984
-around the time of the murder.
-Yes.

113
00:05:50.084 --> 00:05:51.318
It's open-and-shut.

114
00:05:51.418 --> 00:05:53.387
-The file you requested.
Lieutenant.
-Thank you.

115
00:05:58.225 --> 00:06:00.094
-[groans]
-Uh-oh.

116
00:06:00.194 --> 00:06:01.829
You good with that?
Need some help?

117
00:06:01.929 --> 00:06:03.730
No one can help me with this.

118
00:06:04.231 --> 00:06:07.168
I'm gonna have to
rely on someone
I'd rather not deal with.

119
00:06:07.268 --> 00:06:08.768
Hmm.

120
00:06:08.869 --> 00:06:10.237
Welcome to my world.

121
00:06:12.873 --> 00:06:16.844
[Karadec] Dr. Iris Bowman.
tell us about your relationship
with Ernest Lozano.

122
00:06:17.278 --> 00:06:19.380
I wouldn't call it
a relationship.

123
00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:20.848
What would you call it?

124
00:06:20.948 --> 00:06:22.016
[Iris] Harmless fun.

125
00:06:22.116 --> 00:06:25.186
We met three months ago
in a hotel bar and hit it off.

126
00:06:25.286 --> 00:06:27.855
I... I can't believe he's dead.

127
00:06:27.955 --> 00:06:30.024
When you say "hit it off"--

128
00:06:30.124 --> 00:06:31.724
It's exactly
what you think it means.

129
00:06:32.359 --> 00:06:34.195
Ernest would reach out
whenever he was in town.

130
00:06:34.295 --> 00:06:38.698
It was no-strings-attached fun
between two adults.

131
00:06:38.798 --> 00:06:40.733
Hmm. Well. what did
you know about him?

132
00:06:40.834 --> 00:06:43.204
Ernest was a curator
in Mexico City.

133
00:06:43.304 --> 00:06:47.141
He wasn't like most LA guys.
He was fun. charming. cultured.

134
00:06:47.241 --> 00:06:49.376
What's your husband think
about this casual fling?

135
00:06:52.112 --> 00:06:55.049
My husband died in
a car accident nine months ago.

136
00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:58.219
What Ernest and I had
was temporary.

137
00:06:58.319 --> 00:07:01.222
He was meant to be a--
a Band-Aid on my life.

138
00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:02.890
So what happened last night?

139
00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:07.895
Nothing. He was in town.
I met him at our spot.

140
00:07:07.995 --> 00:07:12.399
We had a drink or two.
went up to our room.
drew a bath.

141
00:07:12.499 --> 00:07:14.902
But then Ernest fell asleep.
so I left.

142
00:07:15.002 --> 00:07:16.337
Well. see. the thing is.
Dr. Bowman.

143
00:07:16.437 --> 00:07:18.973
we have you on security footage
entering Lozano's room

144
00:07:19.073 --> 00:07:21.075
and then exiting
30 minutes later.

145
00:07:21.909 --> 00:07:27.747
After that. not a soul enters
or exits that room.
and now Ernest Lozano is dead.

146
00:07:29.749 --> 00:07:31.919
-[stammers]
I told you everything.
-[Karadec] Mm-hmm.

147
00:07:32.019 --> 00:07:33.753
He was alive when I left.

148
00:07:33.854 --> 00:07:35.923
-Do I need a lawyer?
-[Karadec] That would be wise.

149
00:07:36.023 --> 00:07:37.291
You have the right
to remain silent

150
00:07:37.391 --> 00:07:39.927
-and refuse
to answer questions--
-She didn't do it.

151
00:07:40.027 --> 00:07:42.162
The candles. There were
candles lining the bathtub.

152
00:07:42.263 --> 00:07:44.131
I have the same ones at home.
I got 'em on clearance.

153
00:07:44.231 --> 00:07:47.767
They smell like laundry.
But the problem is they only
burn for about four hours.

154
00:07:47.868 --> 00:07:50.271
I'm sorry. I-I don't know.
Is that. uh. a long time?

155
00:07:50.371 --> 00:07:53.507
For a candle? What? No.
A tea light burns longer. No.

156
00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:57.478
All of the candles
were melted down all the way
except for this one.

157
00:07:57.777 --> 00:08:00.981
Something put out this candle.
and you know what puts out fire?

158
00:08:01.081 --> 00:08:02.816
Uh. water.

159
00:08:02.916 --> 00:08:04.451
-Yes. Doctor. Water.
-[Karadec] Water. Water.

160
00:08:04.551 --> 00:08:06.987
[Morgan] <i>Water puts out fire.</i>
<i>In this case. bathwater.</i>

161
00:08:07.087 --> 00:08:09.223
Bathwater put out
this lone candle.

162
00:08:09.323 --> 00:08:11.025
So that means
there was a tub fight.

163
00:08:11.125 --> 00:08:15.129
There was thrashing around.
bathwater splashed out
and extinguished the flame.

164
00:08:15.229 --> 00:08:17.031
<i>The candle looked like</i>
<i>it had been burning</i>

165
00:08:17.131 --> 00:08:18.999
<i>for about an hour and a half.</i>

166
00:08:19.099 --> 00:08:22.503
Okay. So you are saying that
when Dr. Bowman left the room.

167
00:08:22.803 --> 00:08:25.406
all the candles were burning.
and an hour after that.

168
00:08:25.506 --> 00:08:27.474
something happened in that room.

169
00:08:27.574 --> 00:08:29.376
And someone was in that room.

170
00:08:31.078 --> 00:08:32.079
[sighs]

171
00:08:37.818 --> 00:08:38.786
Can I leave now?

172
00:08:39.386 --> 00:08:40.821
[sighs]

173
00:08:41.322 --> 00:08:43.324
[clicks tongue]
Yeah. Free to go.

174
00:08:44.191 --> 00:08:45.326
[sighs]

175
00:08:46.093 --> 00:08:49.163
[door opens. closes]

176
00:08:49.263 --> 00:08:52.066
Speaking of good. huh?

177
00:08:54.168 --> 00:08:56.036
And our only suspect
walks out the door.

178
00:08:58.038 --> 00:09:01.408
Mahomes. under on passing yards.
over on rushing yards.

179
00:09:01.508 --> 00:09:05.446
Uh. next. WNBA Finals.
Aces. moneyline. Wilson.

180
00:09:06.113 --> 00:09:07.281
Ah.

181
00:09:08.582 --> 00:09:11.251
Fifteen years ago.
Roman Sinquerra
painted a mural downtown.

182
00:09:11.352 --> 00:09:16.023
You wrote the citation
and the report.
but both are illegible.

183
00:09:16.123 --> 00:09:17.091
What does this say?

184
00:09:17.191 --> 00:09:21.261
Look. if you're so interested.
give me 2K.

185
00:09:22.129 --> 00:09:25.866
Fine. 1K. [chuckles] No? 500?

186
00:09:25.966 --> 00:09:28.535
I don't care how low you go.
I'm not gonna pay you
for information.

187
00:09:28.635 --> 00:09:29.436
Oh. yeah. Um.

188
00:09:30.904 --> 00:09:33.307
Whatever thing is happening
in NASCAR.
1.000 on numbers ten through 19.

189
00:09:33.407 --> 00:09:36.410
And that's it.
I love you. Mom. Bye.

190
00:09:37.878 --> 00:09:41.382
-Read this address out loud now.
-Hmm.

191
00:09:42.383 --> 00:09:46.420
"14641 Willington Street."

192
00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:47.888
No such place.

193
00:09:47.988 --> 00:09:50.424
There's a Washington Boulevard
and a Billington Street.

194
00:09:50.524 --> 00:09:52.626
but neither has a 14641.

195
00:09:52.893 --> 00:09:55.295
Well. this is definitely
14641 Billington Street.

196
00:09:55.396 --> 00:09:56.630
Again. no such place.

197
00:09:56.897 --> 00:09:58.332
[sighs]

198
00:09:58.432 --> 00:10:00.567
-Fifty bucks says there is.
-There isn't.

199
00:10:01.068 --> 00:10:04.905
But if you wanna give me
your money like that. sure.
I'll check it out.

200
00:10:05.506 --> 00:10:06.507
I'll drive.

201
00:10:14.081 --> 00:10:15.182
Wow.

202
00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:19.420
There used to be nothing
but chop shops and stash houses
on this street.

203
00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:21.088
Now they got a CrossFit gym.

204
00:10:21.188 --> 00:10:24.525
vegan Nashville
hot chicken restaurant
and a third-wave coffee shop.

205
00:10:24.625 --> 00:10:25.659
It's beautiful.

206
00:10:27.361 --> 00:10:30.197
Wait. This was
the street. right?

207
00:10:31.498 --> 00:10:33.967
I could have sworn
the mural was here.

208
00:10:34.935 --> 00:10:36.336
Maybe I didn't write this.

209
00:10:36.437 --> 00:10:37.538
Turn around. Melon.

210
00:10:39.273 --> 00:10:41.608
14614 Billington.

211
00:10:42.676 --> 00:10:45.145
Looks like a sign
protected a part of that mural.

212
00:10:45.245 --> 00:10:48.148
-[camera shutter clicking]
-[Lieutenant Melon]
It's a cool fish tail.

213
00:10:48.248 --> 00:10:51.018
That's a bird's wing.
Those are feathers. not scales.

214
00:10:51.118 --> 00:10:53.053
-That's a fish tail.
-It's a bird's wing.

215
00:10:53.153 --> 00:10:54.288
Double or nothing.

216
00:10:55.189 --> 00:10:56.290
Why not?

217
00:10:58.692 --> 00:11:00.060
Definitely feathers.

218
00:11:05.032 --> 00:11:07.134
-We have news on our victim.
-[Karadec] What's up?

219
00:11:07.234 --> 00:11:10.037
We heard back
from Ernest Lozano's
colleagues in Mexico City.

220
00:11:10.137 --> 00:11:12.339
They say he's alive and well and
showing up at work every day.

221
00:11:12.439 --> 00:11:13.707
-What...
-[Oz] Yeah.

222
00:11:13.974 --> 00:11:15.642
Local police questioned Lozano
about his ID showing up

223
00:11:15.742 --> 00:11:17.644
-at the hotel of a murdered man.
-Mm-hmm.

224
00:11:17.744 --> 00:11:20.414
He told them about a ride
he took a couple years ago
in LA.

225
00:11:20.514 --> 00:11:23.150
On the drive. he and the driver
joked about how much
they looked alike.

226
00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.652
Later that night.
he realized he lost his wallet.
So our victim...

227
00:11:25.752 --> 00:11:27.521
-Is not Ernest Lozano. Okay.
-[Daphne] Yeah.

228
00:11:27.621 --> 00:11:30.157
Put pressure on the lab
for DNA and fingerprints
from the crime scene.

229
00:11:30.257 --> 00:11:32.159
and I'll look more
closely at...

230
00:11:32.259 --> 00:11:33.327
Where's the evidence box?

231
00:11:37.531 --> 00:11:41.502
"Working from home.
See you tomorrow. Morgan."
Smiley face.

232
00:11:45.172 --> 00:11:47.007
[spaceship whirring through TV]

233
00:11:51.011 --> 00:11:53.080
-This is the best
movie night ever.
-[chuckles]

234
00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.351
All right. The trick is to see
if we can notice things
other people can't.

235
00:11:58.185 --> 00:11:59.453
What do you think
these keys go to?

236
00:11:59.553 --> 00:12:02.723
Oh. Bike lock.
PO box and car keys.

237
00:12:04.057 --> 00:12:05.692
-You are my son.
-[fridge door opens]

238
00:12:05.792 --> 00:12:07.227
Ava. can you grab us
a couple of sodas?

239
00:12:07.327 --> 00:12:09.463
I can't touch surfaces
'cause of my gloves.

240
00:12:09.563 --> 00:12:10.631
[fridge door closes]

241
00:12:10.731 --> 00:12:12.366
Oh. Okay. This is interesting.

242
00:12:12.466 --> 00:12:14.034
-Do you see this stitching
right here?
-Mm-hmm.

243
00:12:14.134 --> 00:12:15.536
-Thank you. This is from--
-[person] Two sodas--

244
00:12:15.636 --> 00:12:17.237
[gasps] Oh! Uh.

245
00:12:17.337 --> 00:12:21.308
Hi. strange boy in my house.
Who are you?

246
00:12:21.408 --> 00:12:23.076
I'm Ryder. Ava's friend.

247
00:12:25.546 --> 00:12:26.613
Oh. yeah?

248
00:12:29.716 --> 00:12:31.084
What are you doing?

249
00:12:31.184 --> 00:12:33.487
What does it look like?
Studying.

250
00:12:33.587 --> 00:12:35.389
I couldn't find the cookies.
so I got cheese puffs.

251
00:12:35.489 --> 00:12:37.624
-[Morgan gasps]
-Thank you.
I love cheese puffs--

252
00:12:37.724 --> 00:12:40.594
I bought those. These are mine.
Can I see you in the hallway
for a second?

253
00:12:42.062 --> 00:12:43.063
[Ava sighs]

254
00:12:47.234 --> 00:12:48.435
Okay. Don't freak out.

255
00:12:48.535 --> 00:12:50.237
Ludo said I could have
Ryder in my room

256
00:12:50.337 --> 00:12:52.406
as long as we shut the door
if our music got too loud.

257
00:12:53.407 --> 00:12:57.477
Oh. he did? [laughs]
Man. what a problem-solver.

258
00:12:57.578 --> 00:12:58.745
That's great. because. uh.

259
00:12:58.846 --> 00:13:02.282
my primary concern here was
the decibel level of your music.

260
00:13:02.382 --> 00:13:03.750
No. Mom. we were just studying.

261
00:13:03.851 --> 00:13:05.352
[doorbell rings]

262
00:13:06.119 --> 00:13:07.120
To be continued.

263
00:13:10.524 --> 00:13:11.758
I assume you got my Post-it.

264
00:13:11.859 --> 00:13:13.160
Mmm.

265
00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:15.696
You never take evidence home.
Morgan. Ever. Y--

266
00:13:16.697 --> 00:13:18.198
You have got to be kidding me.

267
00:13:20.133 --> 00:13:22.169
What? He's wearing gloves.

268
00:13:28.508 --> 00:13:31.445
[Morgan] You're upset.
and you're allowed
to have all your feelings.

269
00:13:31.545 --> 00:13:33.614
But people bring their work home
all the time. [chuckles]

270
00:13:33.714 --> 00:13:35.850
My daughter is in her room
working on her homework
right now.

271
00:13:37.618 --> 00:13:40.787
Elliot. go bug your sister.
Quick. Run. Open--
Keep the door open.

272
00:13:40.888 --> 00:13:43.223
[Elliot] Ava.
I'm coming to bug you!

273
00:13:44.558 --> 00:13:47.794
Morgan. this goes against
all rules and all protocol
for handling evidence.

274
00:13:49.663 --> 00:13:51.198
Well. what's the worst
that could happen?

275
00:13:51.298 --> 00:13:53.667
Um. you know. I don't know.
Let me think.

276
00:13:54.401 --> 00:13:56.670
Oh. right. Everything in here
becomes inadmissible in court.

277
00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:58.138
and we can't convict
the murderer.

278
00:13:58.238 --> 00:14:01.375
Yeah. that's bad. That'd be bad.
And I get that.

279
00:14:01.475 --> 00:14:02.844
I did think about that.
But here's the thing.

280
00:14:03.143 --> 00:14:05.212
I really want to help you
figure out who your dead guy is.

281
00:14:05.312 --> 00:14:07.681
[Karadec] Silly me.
I was gonna use dental records
and fingerprints.

282
00:14:07.781 --> 00:14:10.818
There's so many clues
in this evidence box
that you guys missed.

283
00:14:10.918 --> 00:14:13.220
Did anybody even look
at the shoes?

284
00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:14.889
There's cement
all over the soles.

285
00:14:15.155 --> 00:14:17.491
He must have lived or worked
near a construction site.

286
00:14:17.591 --> 00:14:20.828
Oh. Great. We'll just visit
the one construction site
in Los Angeles.

287
00:14:21.896 --> 00:14:25.499
-Okay. Look at the man's pants.
-[Karadec sighs]

288
00:14:25.599 --> 00:14:27.401
This is not
the original stitching.

289
00:14:27.501 --> 00:14:29.236
but this repair was done
by a professional.

290
00:14:29.336 --> 00:14:31.271
-See the-the threads?
-[Karadec] What about them?

291
00:14:31.371 --> 00:14:32.506
[Morgan] They don't cross.

292
00:14:32.606 --> 00:14:34.675
You don't find that
the tiniest bit strange?

293
00:14:34.775 --> 00:14:36.844
Um. I can say
with full confidence
I have never in my life

294
00:14:36.944 --> 00:14:38.545
thought about how threads
cross on a pants hem.

295
00:14:38.645 --> 00:14:41.548
Well. lucky for you. I've spent
a lot of time thinking about it.

296
00:14:41.648 --> 00:14:44.184
The whole hyperactive
brain thing makes it hard
to fall asleep.

297
00:14:44.284 --> 00:14:46.720
So. for a long time.
the only thing
that would put me down

298
00:14:46.821 --> 00:14:48.588
<i>was watching sewing</i>
<i>and alterations videos.</i>

299
00:14:48.689 --> 00:14:50.257
[in Malagasy]

300
00:14:50.724 --> 00:14:53.527
Now. the victim's pants
were repaired without crossing.

301
00:14:54.194 --> 00:14:56.763
That is something that is unique
to tailors from Madagascar.

302
00:14:56.864 --> 00:15:00.367
Because they have a superstition
that crossing your stitches
is unlucky.

303
00:15:00.467 --> 00:15:01.768
Oh. You know what?
Just make a list

304
00:15:01.869 --> 00:15:03.805
of all of the tailors
from Madagascar in the area.

305
00:15:03.905 --> 00:15:06.808
I don't think the LAPD
has a magical database

306
00:15:06.908 --> 00:15:09.376
of seamstresses and tailors
cross-referenced by ethnicity.

307
00:15:09.476 --> 00:15:10.544
But. you know. I can check.

308
00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.949
Well. all you need to do
is find all of the tailor
and alteration shops in LA.

309
00:15:15.215 --> 00:15:16.550
and find the ones
with the longest names.

310
00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:18.785
Because long names
are uniquely Madagascan?

311
00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:23.290
Well. no. but their naming
customs are unlike anywhere else
in the world.

312
00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:26.393
<i>All of their names are unique.</i>
<i>and every name has meaning.</i>

313
00:15:26.493 --> 00:15:27.494
<i>And when two people</i>
<i>get married.</i>

314
00:15:28.996 --> 00:15:30.664
<i>they just smash</i>
<i>their names together</i>
<i>into one big. giant. long name.</i>

315
00:15:30.764 --> 00:15:31.966
<i>It's super romantic.</i>

316
00:15:32.232 --> 00:15:36.904
Okay. So. compile a list
of tailors in Los Angeles.

317
00:15:37.004 --> 00:15:38.405
find the ones
with the longest names.

318
00:15:38.505 --> 00:15:39.907
and then look for one
by a construction site.

319
00:15:41.241 --> 00:15:42.743
Easy peasy lemon squeezy.

320
00:15:42.844 --> 00:15:45.579
Yeah. Okay. Well.
we'll get into that tomorrow.

321
00:15:46.613 --> 00:15:47.982
-Okay.
-[Karadec] And. Morgan.

322
00:15:48.648 --> 00:15:50.250
don't ever use
that phrase again.

323
00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:51.518
Probably will.

324
00:15:51.618 --> 00:15:53.620
[baby crying]

325
00:15:54.755 --> 00:15:55.756
[crying stops]

326
00:15:55.857 --> 00:15:57.357
So. Ryder seems nice.

327
00:15:57.457 --> 00:15:58.860
Funny. That's what his PO said.

328
00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:03.530
[laughs] Parole officer.
Yeah. I get it.
'Cause he was in juvie.

329
00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:05.599
Mm-mmm. Chino.
He was tried as an adult.

330
00:16:05.699 --> 00:16:10.972
You... You are so. so funny.
[laughs]

331
00:16:11.271 --> 00:16:13.540
But seriously.
tell me all about him.
I wanna know.

332
00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:15.943
-Hey. I gotta go
or I'll be late.
-Sit.

333
00:16:16.043 --> 00:16:18.612
Put your phone away.
I guess we're doing this
the hard way.

334
00:16:18.712 --> 00:16:19.881
Did you mean for that to rhyme?

335
00:16:19.981 --> 00:16:22.783
-I will be asking
the questions. Okay?
-[sighs]

336
00:16:22.884 --> 00:16:24.986
-Tell me about Ryder.
-All we were doing was studying.

337
00:16:25.987 --> 00:16:27.955
[sighs] It always
starts with studying.

338
00:16:28.055 --> 00:16:29.790
and then you're lying about
where you were.

339
00:16:29.891 --> 00:16:30.791
And the next thing you know--

340
00:16:32.093 --> 00:16:33.027
You're a single mom.
with three kids.
pretending to be a cop.

341
00:16:36.696 --> 00:16:39.633
I am not pretending.
Thank you very much.
I am consulting.

342
00:16:39.733 --> 00:16:42.302
Oh. yeah. I know.
You don't even have a badge.
[chuckles]

343
00:16:42.402 --> 00:16:45.472
[chuckles] You think
I need a badge? Okay.

344
00:16:46.306 --> 00:16:48.042
I will have you know.
I walk into a space

345
00:16:48.308 --> 00:16:50.477
and people are like.
"Who is this?" Okay?

346
00:16:50.577 --> 00:16:51.545
Then other people are like.

347
00:16:52.612 --> 00:16:53.881
"I don't know. but
she definitely belongs here."

348
00:16:53.981 --> 00:16:55.682
Okay. Mom. this is cringe.
I gotta go.

349
00:16:56.349 --> 00:16:58.485
"Make way." they all yell.
You know?

350
00:16:58.585 --> 00:17:00.320
-"Give that lady access."
-[baby babbles]

351
00:17:01.388 --> 00:17:02.422
[sighs]

352
00:17:04.591 --> 00:17:05.692
When can I get a badge?

353
00:17:06.326 --> 00:17:08.428
Please. stop ringing the bell.
Okay?

354
00:17:09.997 --> 00:17:11.498
How old were you
when you started dating?

355
00:17:12.399 --> 00:17:14.334
I bet you were that guy
who dated the same girl

356
00:17:14.434 --> 00:17:15.669
all four years of high school.

357
00:17:15.769 --> 00:17:17.604
And then on prom night.
you made a pact to lose your--

358
00:17:21.508 --> 00:17:25.345
Hello. Uh. are you
Raissa Rakota Harimalala?

359
00:17:25.445 --> 00:17:27.447
Indeed. You may call me Rai.

360
00:17:27.547 --> 00:17:31.351
Rai. Real quick.
do you recognize this man?

361
00:17:31.451 --> 00:17:32.385
Mr. Eduardo.

362
00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:35.755
-Is-- Is he dead?
-Eduardo is his last name?

363
00:17:36.356 --> 00:17:37.357
No.

364
00:17:37.457 --> 00:17:38.458
Eduardo's his first name.

365
00:17:38.558 --> 00:17:39.526
Yes.

366
00:17:39.626 --> 00:17:40.627
Do you know his last name?

367
00:17:40.727 --> 00:17:42.696
No. Mr. Eduardo.
he always pays in cash.

368
00:17:42.796 --> 00:17:44.031
Can you tell us
anything about him?

369
00:17:45.398 --> 00:17:47.068
Mr. Eduardo
is one of my regulars.

370
00:17:47.367 --> 00:17:49.569
He finds designer suits
secondhand.

371
00:17:49.669 --> 00:17:51.538
I make them look nice again.

372
00:17:56.543 --> 00:17:59.046
-Get excited.
We got a first name.
-Oh. I'm thrilled.

373
00:17:59.146 --> 00:18:01.949
You do realize there could be
thousands of Eduardos
in Los Angeles though. right?

374
00:18:02.049 --> 00:18:05.552
-Wait. Do you hear that?
-[construction drilling]

375
00:18:05.652 --> 00:18:07.922
Sweet. sweet sounds of the city.

376
00:18:10.724 --> 00:18:13.828
Okay. Tailor shop.
construction site. Now what?

377
00:18:13.928 --> 00:18:16.097
-Now we just got to find
a parking lot.
-Mmm.

378
00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:18.632
Yep. Right here.

379
00:18:18.732 --> 00:18:20.067
Why do we care about parking?

380
00:18:20.167 --> 00:18:21.468
Don't get mad at me.

381
00:18:21.936 --> 00:18:23.403
Of course you brought his keys.

382
00:18:23.503 --> 00:18:24.839
Well. it's still in
the evidence bag.

383
00:18:24.939 --> 00:18:27.474
and it's a good thing I did.
Because...

384
00:18:27.909 --> 00:18:29.143
[car beeping]

385
00:18:31.078 --> 00:18:33.848
[Morgan] Eduardo's Toyota.

386
00:18:34.916 --> 00:18:35.917
Next time.

387
00:18:36.616 --> 00:18:37.651
[camera shutter clicking]

388
00:18:38.752 --> 00:18:39.753
[dialing]

389
00:18:39.854 --> 00:18:40.988
[ringing tone]

390
00:18:41.088 --> 00:18:42.422
[Daphne] <i>Forester.</i>
<i>What do you need?</i>

391
00:18:43.991 --> 00:18:44.959
Hey. Daph. I just sent you the
plates on our victim's vehicle.
Do your thing.

392
00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:47.128
Probably the car Ernest Lozano
lost his wallet in.

393
00:18:47.427 --> 00:18:49.163
And I will have to
call you back. Bye.

394
00:18:49.429 --> 00:18:51.165
Morgan. Morgan.
No. No. no. Don't.

395
00:18:51.933 --> 00:18:53.433
Gloves. Sorry. Gloves.

396
00:18:53.533 --> 00:18:56.103
No. it's not the gloves.
Actually. yes.
you do need gloves.

397
00:18:56.203 --> 00:18:58.772
But before you touch anything.
just ask me first. Okay?

398
00:18:58.873 --> 00:19:00.107
No. you're totally right.
I apologize.

399
00:19:00.207 --> 00:19:01.943
You said that before.
Won't happen again.

400
00:19:02.043 --> 00:19:03.945
-Mmm.
-Except I did
already touch everything.

401
00:19:04.045 --> 00:19:07.547
He's got suits. charging cables.
little waters. hand--

402
00:19:07.647 --> 00:19:08.883
The guy's a rideshare driver.

403
00:19:08.983 --> 00:19:11.484
Yes. How'd you do that so fast?

404
00:19:11.584 --> 00:19:13.520
Easy peasy lemon squeezy.

405
00:19:16.623 --> 00:19:19.459
Oh. Yep. Yeah. that too.

406
00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:25.532
Daphne says the car is
registered to an Eduardo Cortez.

407
00:19:25.632 --> 00:19:26.633
lives in this building.

408
00:19:27.935 --> 00:19:29.170
Ten years ago. the guy did time
in prison for credit card fraud.

409
00:19:29.469 --> 00:19:31.172
Since then.
he's been a rideshare driver.

410
00:19:31.973 --> 00:19:34.541
Pretty fancy building
for a driver.
We sure this is our guy?

411
00:19:34.641 --> 00:19:35.977
Mmm.

412
00:19:36.077 --> 00:19:38.045
-Eduardo Cortez's
driver's license.
-Whew.

413
00:19:38.145 --> 00:19:39.814
[Morgan] Yeah. that's him.

414
00:19:39.914 --> 00:19:41.983
How do you have
a hot driver's license photo?

415
00:19:42.984 --> 00:19:44.484
216. this should be the place.

416
00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:49.023
-Morgan.
what did I tell you ab--
-I'm just kidding. [laughs]

417
00:19:50.757 --> 00:19:51.758
You're fun.

418
00:19:52.492 --> 00:19:53.760
-You're a lot of fun.
-Mm-hmm.

419
00:19:53.861 --> 00:19:55.762
[child] Daddy! Daddy's home!

420
00:19:57.999 --> 00:20:00.500
Hello? Can I help you?

421
00:20:01.002 --> 00:20:03.770
Sorry to intrude. ma'am.
I'm LAPD Detective Adam Karadec.

422
00:20:03.871 --> 00:20:05.605
This is consultant
Morgan Gillery.

423
00:20:06.207 --> 00:20:08.175
Do you happen to know
an Eduardo Cortez?

424
00:20:08.275 --> 00:20:09.542
He's my husband.

425
00:20:10.777 --> 00:20:12.013
Is he okay?

426
00:20:17.184 --> 00:20:19.786
Cheers. [sips] Ooh!

427
00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:22.522
I'll let mine cool down.
It's just too hot.

428
00:20:22.622 --> 00:20:24.724
Gosh. Thank you. guys.
for sharing your tea with me.

429
00:20:24.825 --> 00:20:26.093
Your friends
are all really nice.

430
00:20:26.193 --> 00:20:27.761
Do you have a favorite friend?

431
00:20:27.862 --> 00:20:29.529
[whispers]
I won't tell the rest.

432
00:20:29.629 --> 00:20:32.066
Stanley. He can take photos too.

433
00:20:32.166 --> 00:20:35.870
Stanley can take photos?
No way. Let me see this.

434
00:20:36.804 --> 00:20:37.972
So cool.

435
00:20:38.605 --> 00:20:42.642
My daddy gave him to me.
He also gave me that dictionary.

436
00:20:42.742 --> 00:20:46.881
Oh. Words are--
Words are important.

437
00:20:48.249 --> 00:20:50.717
[Eduardo's spouse]
We've been together
for ten years.

438
00:20:50.818 --> 00:20:54.088
I met Eduardo after
he was released from prison.

439
00:20:54.188 --> 00:20:55.256
How long had he been driving?

440
00:20:55.555 --> 00:20:57.557
He used to drive
up until three years ago.

441
00:20:57.657 --> 00:21:00.593
when he started at his cousin's
wine distribution business.

442
00:21:00.693 --> 00:21:02.463
Was it strange that
he hadn't come home?

443
00:21:02.595 --> 00:21:04.731
His job required
quite a bit of travel.

444
00:21:04.832 --> 00:21:06.934
so Eduardo being away
felt normal.

445
00:21:08.903 --> 00:21:10.670
I can't believe he's gone.

446
00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:12.073
Do you recognize this woman?

447
00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:15.976
I don't. Who is she?

448
00:21:16.077 --> 00:21:20.247
I'm sorry. but Eduardo was
not traveling for work.

449
00:21:20.347 --> 00:21:23.017
We found his body
in a Hollywood hotel room.

450
00:21:23.117 --> 00:21:25.585
He was with this woman
the night he was murdered.

451
00:21:33.693 --> 00:21:36.263
What the hell was that?
Woman just found out
her husband died.

452
00:21:36.363 --> 00:21:38.765
Maybe give it a sec
before you whip out
a picture of his mistress.

453
00:21:38.866 --> 00:21:40.667
If Regina knew
about Eduardo's infidelity.

454
00:21:40.767 --> 00:21:42.036
she had strong motive
to kill him.

455
00:21:42.136 --> 00:21:44.138
I needed to get
her unvarnished reaction.

456
00:21:44.238 --> 00:21:46.773
Ooh. That's good.
Didn't think about that.

457
00:21:46.874 --> 00:21:50.211
It seems like
there's a lot about this job
you don't think about.

458
00:21:50.311 --> 00:21:51.778
You know. like taking evidence
home with you.

459
00:21:51.879 --> 00:21:54.215
You know. every once in a while
I start to get tricked

460
00:21:54.315 --> 00:21:56.183
into thinking that maybe
you're a normal person.

461
00:21:56.283 --> 00:21:59.320
And then. sure enough.
you remind me you're just
a heartless police robot.

462
00:21:59.652 --> 00:22:00.620
Mm-hmm.

463
00:22:01.188 --> 00:22:02.655
Why would Eduardo use a fake ID?

464
00:22:02.755 --> 00:22:05.725
Why would someone change
their entire identity
just to cheat on their wife?

465
00:22:05.826 --> 00:22:06.894
Doesn't add up.

466
00:22:06.994 --> 00:22:08.896
-Finances don't really
add up either.
-Mmm.

467
00:22:08.996 --> 00:22:12.299
Sweet apartment. the car.
altering suits to make 'em
look expensive.

468
00:22:12.399 --> 00:22:15.970
Yep. Go home. get rest and
do not take evidence with you.

469
00:22:18.806 --> 00:22:20.007
[Ludo] It was
a pretty normal day.

470
00:22:20.107 --> 00:22:21.641
Ava was glued to her phone.

471
00:22:21.741 --> 00:22:24.044
Elliot worked on
his geography project.

472
00:22:24.145 --> 00:22:26.147
Chloe ate a crayon.
but it was a green one.

473
00:22:26.247 --> 00:22:27.747
So I feel it was
the most natural
and least toxic.

474
00:22:27.848 --> 00:22:29.350
Did Chloe have a guest?

475
00:22:30.851 --> 00:22:33.387
Chloe. the baby.
did not have a guest.

476
00:22:33.686 --> 00:22:36.756
She didn't have any. uh.
floppy-haired teenage boys
swing by?

477
00:22:36.857 --> 00:22:39.326
[sighs] I knew this was coming.
Look. Ryder seems like
a nice kid.

478
00:22:39.426 --> 00:22:40.693
-I think it's fine.
-It's not fine.

479
00:22:42.062 --> 00:22:43.831
We both knew that she was gonna
have friends over
sooner or later.

480
00:22:43.931 --> 00:22:46.699
-Ava's 15.
-What were you doing
when you were 15?

481
00:22:46.799 --> 00:22:48.269
Lifting. Hanging with my boys.

482
00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:51.704
I'm kidding.
I didn't lift or have boys.
But Ava's different.

483
00:22:51.805 --> 00:22:54.408
I know Ava's different.
but teenage boys
are all the same.

484
00:22:54.674 --> 00:22:56.410
Well. we're both trying
to figure it out. okay?

485
00:22:56.676 --> 00:22:58.212
I think we just need
to trust Ava.

486
00:22:58.312 --> 00:23:00.347
-So let's calm down. and--
-Don't tell me to calm down.

487
00:23:00.447 --> 00:23:03.284
-You overstepped in a major way.
-Okay.

488
00:23:03.384 --> 00:23:06.220
You're on one of your famous
Morgan rants. so I'm gonna
give you a little space.

489
00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:08.088
I am not on a Morgan rant.

490
00:23:08.189 --> 00:23:11.859
I am doing what's best
for my kid. Ava is my daughter.

491
00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:15.863
Right. I'm just the manny.

492
00:23:17.398 --> 00:23:18.765
That's not what I said.

493
00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:21.368
That's not what I meant.
You don't-- [sighs]

494
00:23:21.969 --> 00:23:24.238
Can you please come back here
so we can--

495
00:23:24.737 --> 00:23:25.906
Okay.

496
00:23:26.006 --> 00:23:27.141
[door slams]

497
00:23:31.412 --> 00:23:33.314
Eduardo's life
just doesn't add up.

498
00:23:33.414 --> 00:23:35.416
I'm late. I'm sorry.
Sorry I'm late.

499
00:23:35.715 --> 00:23:37.750
It was a crazy kid morning.
but who cares about that?

500
00:23:37.851 --> 00:23:38.751
Who cares about kids?

501
00:23:38.852 --> 00:23:40.221
-I brought doughnuts.
-Hell yeah.

502
00:23:40.321 --> 00:23:41.388
I love it when you're late.

503
00:23:41.989 --> 00:23:44.091
Ooh. So many options.

504
00:23:44.191 --> 00:23:45.792
Can't go wrong
with glaze. right?

505
00:23:45.893 --> 00:23:47.394
-Never.
-[Oz] No.

506
00:23:47.494 --> 00:23:48.728
Maybe it's a sprinkles day.

507
00:23:50.097 --> 00:23:50.898
-Might be a sprinkles day.
-Yeah. I don't know.
Is that a jelly--

508
00:23:50.998 --> 00:23:53.234
-Just pick a damn doughnut.
-Okay.

509
00:23:53.334 --> 00:23:55.735
[Selena] Medical examiner's
report just came in.

510
00:23:55.836 --> 00:23:58.472
Eduardo Cortez's cause of death
is drowning

511
00:23:58.738 --> 00:24:00.474
with trace amounts of alcohol
in his system.

512
00:24:00.740 --> 00:24:03.244
But he looked sober
on the CCTV footage
when he was walking with Iris.

513
00:24:03.344 --> 00:24:04.912
Bartender says he tied one on.

514
00:24:05.012 --> 00:24:07.147
Could have been all the Xanax
the lab found in his system.

515
00:24:07.248 --> 00:24:08.983
They also found residue
on the glassware.

516
00:24:09.083 --> 00:24:12.920
Well. Xanax's main side effects
are severe drowsiness
and dizziness.

517
00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:16.957
Still. no one other than
Dr. Bowman used the key card
to enter the room that night.

518
00:24:17.057 --> 00:24:18.492
But the cameras proved
she isn't the killer.

519
00:24:20.094 --> 00:24:24.098
What if the murderer
entered the room in a way
that didn't require a key card?

520
00:24:24.198 --> 00:24:26.033
What are you talking about.
Morgan?

521
00:24:26.133 --> 00:24:29.003
I'm talking about the murderer
going into the room
through the window.

522
00:24:29.103 --> 00:24:30.337
The room is on the fourth floor.

523
00:24:30.437 --> 00:24:32.273
You'd have to be Spider-Man.

524
00:24:32.373 --> 00:24:34.774
Really. Oz? I brought doughnuts.

525
00:24:35.276 --> 00:24:38.445
Look. would it be easy? No.
But is it doable? I think so.

526
00:24:39.213 --> 00:24:40.447
Right here.

527
00:24:40.547 --> 00:24:42.449
I didn't see it
when I was there in person.

528
00:24:42.549 --> 00:24:44.852
There was
a crime scene photographer
standing in front of me.

529
00:24:44.952 --> 00:24:46.954
But... do you see?

530
00:24:47.054 --> 00:24:49.323
-What are we looking at?
-The window latches.

531
00:24:49.423 --> 00:24:51.825
See? All of those are turned
to the left except for this one.

532
00:24:51.925 --> 00:24:54.028
which is turned to the right.
which means it's unlocked.

533
00:24:54.794 --> 00:24:57.965
The murderer could have
done the deed and then exited
through the window

534
00:24:58.065 --> 00:24:59.967
but not been able
to lock it from the outside.

535
00:25:00.067 --> 00:25:02.703
Except it's on
the fourth floor. Morgan.

536
00:25:02.836 --> 00:25:04.171
What other leads do we have?

537
00:25:04.838 --> 00:25:06.307
Cortez's financials.

538
00:25:06.407 --> 00:25:08.142
A prelim check
showed he had no debt.

539
00:25:08.242 --> 00:25:10.144
Lived in a fancy apartment.
Drove a beat-up car.

540
00:25:10.244 --> 00:25:13.347
Yeah. He did wear old suits and
doctor 'em up to look expensive.

541
00:25:13.447 --> 00:25:15.516
All right. Keep digging
into his finances.

542
00:25:15.816 --> 00:25:17.818
Oz. check out
Morgan's window theory.

543
00:25:17.918 --> 00:25:19.486
[whispers] Yes!

544
00:25:23.424 --> 00:25:24.825
Here you go. sir.

545
00:25:24.925 --> 00:25:25.993
-Thanks.
-Mm-hmm.

546
00:25:31.565 --> 00:25:33.200
[vehicles passing]

547
00:25:34.234 --> 00:25:36.337
♪ <i>Spider-Man. Spider-Man</i> ♪

548
00:25:36.437 --> 00:25:39.039
♪ <i>Does whatever a spider can</i> ♪

549
00:25:39.139 --> 00:25:40.507
♪ <i>Look out!</i> ♪

550
00:25:40.607 --> 00:25:42.910
♪ <i>Here comes the Spider-Man</i> ♪

551
00:25:45.913 --> 00:25:46.914
[camera shutter clicking]

552
00:25:47.014 --> 00:25:49.383
It was a yellow light.
You could have just gone.

553
00:25:49.483 --> 00:25:51.518
[Oz] Hey. Big news.

554
00:25:51.618 --> 00:25:53.354
I just got back from the hotel.

555
00:25:53.454 --> 00:25:56.056
Manager said it's policy for
the cleaners to lock the windows

556
00:25:56.156 --> 00:25:57.591
when they finish cleaning
the room each morning.

557
00:25:57.858 --> 00:26:00.527
So if a window was unlocked.
guest must have
done it that day.

558
00:26:00.627 --> 00:26:02.229
-So it was left unlocked.
-[Oz] Mm-hmm.

559
00:26:02.329 --> 00:26:04.932
But the question wasn't
whether the window was unlocked.

560
00:26:05.032 --> 00:26:07.434
-Could someone
get up to the window?
-[Oz] I think someone could.

561
00:26:07.534 --> 00:26:09.403
Not me but someone.

562
00:26:09.503 --> 00:26:13.974
Look. there are recessed windows
and a lot of ledges.

563
00:26:14.074 --> 00:26:17.311
In between those.
crevices and indentations.

564
00:26:17.411 --> 00:26:19.546
But check this out.

565
00:26:20.881 --> 00:26:22.149
[Karadec] What is that? Powder?

566
00:26:22.249 --> 00:26:24.885
Yes. it's powder.
It's magnesium carbonate.

567
00:26:24.985 --> 00:26:27.154
-That's the powder climbers use.
-[Daphne] Hey. guys.

568
00:26:27.254 --> 00:26:28.489
Okay. Thank you.

569
00:26:30.057 --> 00:26:32.559
You wanted a deep dive
into Eduardo Cortez's money?

570
00:26:32.659 --> 00:26:35.963
I just learned
he deposited checks
totaling more than 200.000

571
00:26:36.063 --> 00:26:38.298
in the past year from
a woman named Glenda Walker.

572
00:26:38.399 --> 00:26:39.900
Her address.

573
00:26:46.173 --> 00:26:47.574
This is it.

574
00:26:47.674 --> 00:26:49.309
Feel like
an assisted-living community

575
00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:52.446
isn't a hotbed
for criminal activity.

576
00:26:53.247 --> 00:26:54.248
[knocking on door]

577
00:26:56.917 --> 00:26:58.652
LAPD.
Is this Glenda Walker's room?

578
00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:00.521
Uh. yeah.

579
00:27:00.621 --> 00:27:02.089
Can we ask her some questions?

580
00:27:03.257 --> 00:27:04.591
You can try.

581
00:27:08.095 --> 00:27:10.497
[Karadec] Glenda.
do you know Eduardo Cortez?

582
00:27:12.933 --> 00:27:16.170
I am sorry. I-I don't. Should I?

583
00:27:17.538 --> 00:27:20.441
Uh. what about Ernest Lozano?

584
00:27:21.208 --> 00:27:23.010
Oh. yes. [chuckles]

585
00:27:23.110 --> 00:27:25.045
Ernest is my fiancé.

586
00:27:25.512 --> 00:27:28.115
We made plans to fly
to Mexico City.

587
00:27:28.215 --> 00:27:30.317
He's picking me up
any minute now.

588
00:27:30.951 --> 00:27:31.952
[Karadec] Oh.

589
00:27:35.622 --> 00:27:37.691
Can I ask why
you gave him $200.000?

590
00:27:38.559 --> 00:27:40.127
She what?

591
00:27:40.994 --> 00:27:43.564
Mom? Is that true?

592
00:27:43.664 --> 00:27:46.333
It's for our home
in Mexico City.

593
00:27:46.433 --> 00:27:49.636
I gave Ernest my half. and
he is taking care of everything.

594
00:27:49.736 --> 00:27:52.239
It has a lovely guest room
just for Kyle.

595
00:27:52.339 --> 00:27:53.407
Oh. [chuckles]

596
00:27:58.312 --> 00:28:00.481
Kyle. do you mind
if we speak in private?

597
00:28:02.015 --> 00:28:03.217
Yeah.

598
00:28:06.186 --> 00:28:08.021
[sighs] How long
has she been like this?

599
00:28:08.655 --> 00:28:10.991
My mom got diagnosed
almost a year ago.

600
00:28:12.426 --> 00:28:14.995
It's getting worse.
Doctors say it's dementia.

601
00:28:15.095 --> 00:28:17.664
Yeah. You ever meet this Ernest?

602
00:28:17.764 --> 00:28:21.168
No. No. I was living up north
when they first met.

603
00:28:21.268 --> 00:28:24.671
So. my mom got sick.
Stopped hearing from the guy.

604
00:28:27.741 --> 00:28:31.345
-[knocking on door]
-[vacuum cleaner switches off]

605
00:28:38.352 --> 00:28:40.521
We're gonna have to establish
some boundaries.

606
00:28:40.621 --> 00:28:42.356
'cause Karadec was just here
the other night.

607
00:28:42.456 --> 00:28:44.057
I have a Roman question.

608
00:28:45.292 --> 00:28:46.560
So this.

609
00:28:47.227 --> 00:28:49.263
-[Morgan] Roman's work.
-[Selena] Mm-hmm.

610
00:28:49.363 --> 00:28:50.531
Bird's wing or fishtail?

611
00:28:51.798 --> 00:28:53.133
Please tell me
it's a bird's wing.
I got 50 bucks on it.

612
00:28:53.233 --> 00:28:55.035
-It's a bird's wing.
-Yes!

613
00:28:55.135 --> 00:28:57.037
It's a Phoenix actually.
Do you wanna see
the whole thing?

614
00:28:57.137 --> 00:28:58.705
I would. in fact.

615
00:29:00.407 --> 00:29:02.543
Is this relevant to anything
other than your bet?

616
00:29:03.277 --> 00:29:04.545
A month before he went missing.

617
00:29:05.947 --> 00:29:08.482
Roman was cited
for painting a mural
in an unauthorized location.

618
00:29:08.582 --> 00:29:11.618
Back then. the area was
a hotbed for criminal activity.

619
00:29:11.718 --> 00:29:15.055
-Roman wasn't a criminal.
-I wonder if he saw
something in the area

620
00:29:15.155 --> 00:29:16.456
-he shouldn't have.
-I don't know.

621
00:29:16.557 --> 00:29:19.393
He didn't say anything about it.
I'm sure he would have.

622
00:29:19.493 --> 00:29:20.627
Oh. wow.

623
00:29:21.495 --> 00:29:23.564
-Isn't that cool?
-It really is.

624
00:29:23.664 --> 00:29:26.066
Keep it. Keep all of them.
They might be useful.

625
00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:28.435
-Great.
-Do you want a cookie?

626
00:29:29.636 --> 00:29:31.672
I'm not sure if that's
a serious question or not.

627
00:29:31.772 --> 00:29:35.108
-But if it is. yes.
-I don't joke around
about cookies.

628
00:29:44.117 --> 00:29:45.319
I'm not concerned
about your bowels.

629
00:29:45.419 --> 00:29:47.521
I just hide the good stuff
where the kids will never look.

630
00:29:49.089 --> 00:29:50.090
[chuckles]

631
00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:51.491
You are a genius.

632
00:29:52.292 --> 00:29:53.828
Had I known this
when I was raising my kids.

633
00:29:54.094 --> 00:29:56.163
my books would've been
full with cookies.

634
00:29:57.097 --> 00:29:58.265
Mmm.

635
00:29:58.365 --> 00:30:01.168
I bet you could fit a lot
of cookies in a dictionary.

636
00:30:10.110 --> 00:30:12.379
Thank you for letting me look
at your daddy's gift.

637
00:30:21.788 --> 00:30:24.725
-[sighs]
-Oh. my. How much is in there?

638
00:30:24.826 --> 00:30:27.461
Ballpark. Fifty grand.

639
00:30:28.462 --> 00:30:30.230
Where did Eduardo
get this money?

640
00:30:30.797 --> 00:30:31.798
Working theory.

641
00:30:33.233 --> 00:30:35.302
he was seducing vulnerable women
and scamming them
out of their money.

642
00:30:35.402 --> 00:30:38.572
Take it away.
Give it back to those women.

643
00:30:38.672 --> 00:30:40.707
Wanna see my Stanley photos?

644
00:30:40.808 --> 00:30:44.645
I sure do want to see
your Stanley photos.
[chuckles] Oh. cute.

645
00:30:48.348 --> 00:30:49.483
That's my daddy.

646
00:30:56.423 --> 00:30:58.191
-There he is.
-Here I am.

647
00:30:58.291 --> 00:30:59.559
-Saved you a stool.
-Thank you.

648
00:31:00.327 --> 00:31:01.662
-Your. uh. drink's on the way.
-Hmm.

649
00:31:01.762 --> 00:31:03.764
Dirty vodka martini
on the rocks.

650
00:31:03.865 --> 00:31:06.400
-Hmm.
-Weird drink for my weird guy.

651
00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:09.269
Yeah. well. martini glasses
are what's weird.

652
00:31:09.369 --> 00:31:12.372
It's poorly designed barware.
Terrible for people
with large hands.

653
00:31:12.472 --> 00:31:13.908
And how did you guess my drink?

654
00:31:14.174 --> 00:31:15.910
I've been cleaning up
in major crimes for months.

655
00:31:16.176 --> 00:31:17.411
I know you better than
you know yourself.

656
00:31:17.511 --> 00:31:18.913
-Oh. you do?
-I do.

657
00:31:19.179 --> 00:31:20.614
Okay. Well. tell me
about myself then.

658
00:31:22.182 --> 00:31:24.852
You fill your car up every
two weeks at the same spot
next to Union Station.

659
00:31:24.952 --> 00:31:26.888
You love yourself a French dip
from Philippe's.

660
00:31:27.187 --> 00:31:28.789
-You avoid the 405 at all costs.
-Mm-hmm.

661
00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:32.793
And. uh. your favorite doughnut
is not a doughnut.
It's an apple fritter.

662
00:31:32.894 --> 00:31:33.760
Hmm.

663
00:31:33.861 --> 00:31:35.562
So where was my fritter earlier?

664
00:31:36.196 --> 00:31:37.865
I haven't decided
if I like you yet.

665
00:31:37.965 --> 00:31:41.735
Hmm. What was so important
that we couldn't talk about it
on the phone?

666
00:31:41.836 --> 00:31:42.937
Yes.

667
00:31:44.571 --> 00:31:47.274
So we know that Eduardo was
conning rich. vulnerable women
to get their money.

668
00:31:47.374 --> 00:31:51.211
And who meets a lot of rich.
vulnerable women?

669
00:31:56.416 --> 00:31:58.485
-A hotel bartender.
-[Morgan] Mm-hmm.

670
00:32:00.454 --> 00:32:01.889
-[Karadec] Hey!
-Ah. Oh.

671
00:32:11.264 --> 00:32:12.299
[alarm blaring]

672
00:32:14.434 --> 00:32:15.635
[stammers] What are you doing?

673
00:32:15.736 --> 00:32:16.971
I'm not going back to prison.

674
00:32:17.237 --> 00:32:18.772
Oz. put the gun down.

675
00:32:20.942 --> 00:32:22.576
[grunts. groaning]

676
00:32:29.416 --> 00:32:30.985
-[Morgan] Yeah.
they went out the front.
-Morgan!

677
00:32:31.251 --> 00:32:32.619
-Check the back door.
-Yes. ma'am.

678
00:32:33.487 --> 00:32:35.455
Why didn't you tell me
what I was walking into?

679
00:32:35.555 --> 00:32:38.926
-I didn't know
all that was gonna happen.
-I could've gotten stabbed.

680
00:32:39.026 --> 00:32:41.461
Okay. I think you're
overreacting just a teeny bit.

681
00:32:41.561 --> 00:32:44.331
I am not overreacting.
We both could've been
seriously hurt.

682
00:32:44.431 --> 00:32:47.601
But we didn't. right?
And how about a little bit
of gratitude. by the way?

683
00:32:47.701 --> 00:32:49.669
Oh. gratitude.
Gratitude for what?

684
00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:51.538
I gave you a break in the case.

685
00:32:51.638 --> 00:32:54.274
Oh! Thank you. Morgan.
for giving me a break
in the case

686
00:32:54.374 --> 00:32:55.776
in the most idiotic way
possible.

687
00:32:55.877 --> 00:32:59.613
How can someone as smart as you
are do so many stupid things?

688
00:32:59.713 --> 00:33:03.683
[scoffs] Why does everyone
feel like they have the right
to just call me stupid?

689
00:33:03.784 --> 00:33:06.486
I don't have to take this.
I don't need this job that bad.

690
00:33:06.586 --> 00:33:09.289
-Fine. This job
does not need you.
-Perfect. I quit.

691
00:33:17.965 --> 00:33:20.034
-There is no way Morgan quit.
-That's what I heard.

692
00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:22.636
-She stormed off at the hotel.
-[elevator dings]

693
00:33:22.736 --> 00:33:23.737
Karadec. [clears throat]

694
00:33:23.838 --> 00:33:25.006
Daphne. meet me
in Interrogation.

695
00:33:25.305 --> 00:33:26.974
Oz. get started
on the paperwork.

696
00:33:28.475 --> 00:33:29.509
[sighs]

697
00:33:30.845 --> 00:33:35.515
Assault. battery. extortion.
buying and selling
of stolen goods.

698
00:33:35.615 --> 00:33:36.984
And Eduardo Cortez's murder.

699
00:33:37.084 --> 00:33:38.820
We know you guys
shared a cell in prison.

700
00:33:38.920 --> 00:33:41.989
What was your relationship like
with Eduardo
after being released?

701
00:33:43.323 --> 00:33:46.027
Look. you pulled
a knife on a cop.

702
00:33:46.326 --> 00:33:48.863
You wanna minimize
your jail time.
this is your chance.

703
00:33:50.998 --> 00:33:54.001
[stammers] When I got out.
I was doing okay bartending.

704
00:33:54.101 --> 00:33:57.571
But I knew that
Eduardo was struggling.

705
00:33:57.671 --> 00:34:00.842
He helped me on the inside.
so I felt like I owed him.

706
00:34:00.942 --> 00:34:03.010
When I started at the hotel.
he came up with a plan.

707
00:34:03.110 --> 00:34:04.444
To exploit vulnerable women.

708
00:34:04.544 --> 00:34:06.013
I only gave him possible marks.

709
00:34:06.113 --> 00:34:09.016
After that. whatever Eduardo did
with these women is on him.

710
00:34:09.116 --> 00:34:10.517
But you got a finder's fee.

711
00:34:10.617 --> 00:34:12.619
Sure. but I had
nothing to do with his death.

712
00:34:12.719 --> 00:34:14.856
Why would I want Eduardo killed?

713
00:34:14.956 --> 00:34:17.859
He was my goose
that laid golden eggs.

714
00:34:25.867 --> 00:34:27.869
[clicks tongue]

715
00:34:27.969 --> 00:34:30.537
Prado admitted to running a con
with our victim.

716
00:34:30.637 --> 00:34:32.039
-but he lawyered up. so...
-[sighs]

717
00:34:32.907 --> 00:34:33.908
Any other leads?

718
00:34:34.008 --> 00:34:36.543
Yeah. a few.
I'll chase 'em down.

719
00:34:37.377 --> 00:34:41.448
Oh. uh... One more thing.
Morgan quit.

720
00:34:42.782 --> 00:34:43.783
She didn't quit.

721
00:34:43.885 --> 00:34:45.887
Mmm. No.
she made it pretty clear.

722
00:34:45.987 --> 00:34:47.554
I chewed her out
after the hotel incident.

723
00:34:47.654 --> 00:34:49.723
and she stormed off yelling
she didn't need the job.

724
00:34:49.824 --> 00:34:53.560
She'll be back. She needs this.

725
00:35:00.600 --> 00:35:01.801
[Ludo] You quit?

726
00:35:03.237 --> 00:35:05.940
Well. he was yelling.
I was yelling.
I don't know. I felt attacked.

727
00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:08.943
-I really liked that job.
-Liked? I loved that job.

728
00:35:09.043 --> 00:35:12.445
It was a sweet gig.
and they paid me for childcare.

729
00:35:12.545 --> 00:35:15.515
So. wait. why did you call me
all the way over here
if you didn't need a manny?

730
00:35:16.083 --> 00:35:17.985
[sighs] I just wanted
to tell you in person

731
00:35:18.085 --> 00:35:21.488
that I'm really sorry about what
I said to you the other night.

732
00:35:22.422 --> 00:35:24.591
You're the closest thing
Ava has to a father.

733
00:35:24.691 --> 00:35:26.928
and we're all really lucky
to have you in our lives.

734
00:35:29.462 --> 00:35:30.865
Thanks.

735
00:35:32.799 --> 00:35:35.435
Now. if you can just apologize
like that to the LAPD.

736
00:35:35.535 --> 00:35:38.039
I'm pretty sure you could
get that sweet job back.

737
00:35:38.139 --> 00:35:40.774
LAPD are bossy. You know
how I feel about authority.

738
00:35:40.875 --> 00:35:42.642
[chuckles] I do know.

739
00:35:42.742 --> 00:35:44.444
Hot Dog on a Stick.

740
00:35:44.544 --> 00:35:46.713
H&R Block. Magic Castle.

741
00:35:46.814 --> 00:35:49.783
Kaiser Permanente Sunset.
Kaiser Permanente Culver City.

742
00:35:49.884 --> 00:35:52.119
-Kaiser Permanente--
-I get it. I quit a lot.

743
00:35:53.154 --> 00:35:54.188
Well. we'll figure it out.

744
00:35:55.488 --> 00:35:58.125
Although. I do think I should
tell you. yesterday at pickup.

745
00:35:58.225 --> 00:36:01.963
I heard Elliot
bragging to his classmates
about his mom being a detective.

746
00:36:02.629 --> 00:36:05.565
I've never heard Elliot boasting
about his driving instructor dad

747
00:36:05.665 --> 00:36:07.534
or his cleaning lady mom.
have you?

748
00:36:09.703 --> 00:36:12.472
We gotta tell him
to stop bragging
about his mom being a cop.

749
00:36:12.572 --> 00:36:14.674
-Everyone's gonna think
he's a narc.
-[chuckles]

750
00:36:15.977 --> 00:36:17.644
Thanks for the apology.

751
00:36:18.179 --> 00:36:19.213
Bye.

752
00:36:20.047 --> 00:36:21.481
[Chloe babbles]

753
00:36:34.494 --> 00:36:36.563
[Daphne]
Prado's lawyer made it clear.
there's no confession coming.

754
00:36:36.663 --> 00:36:38.498
Yeah. it's fine.
He's not our guy.

755
00:36:38.598 --> 00:36:40.533
Couldn't have been
in the hotel room
at the time of death.

756
00:36:40.633 --> 00:36:42.602
Staff confirmed his alibi. and
we're getting security footage

757
00:36:42.702 --> 00:36:45.206
-from the downstairs bar--
-And his nails.

758
00:36:45.505 --> 00:36:47.208
-I told you.
-[Daphne] Thanks.

759
00:36:47.507 --> 00:36:49.010
I know which one I want.

760
00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:51.212
Can we please
get back to the case?

761
00:36:51.511 --> 00:36:53.713
Yes. As I was saying.
his fingernails.

762
00:36:53.814 --> 00:36:57.018
What about 'em?
The victim wasn't
scratched to death. Morgan.

763
00:36:57.118 --> 00:36:58.219
No. he was not.

764
00:36:58.518 --> 00:37:01.489
But. in order to climb
the exterior of a hotel

765
00:37:01.621 --> 00:37:03.024
and get into
the fourth-floor window.

766
00:37:03.124 --> 00:37:04.691
the murderer would have to
have short nails.

767
00:37:04.791 --> 00:37:07.028
The bartender had long.
flamenco-guitar nails.

768
00:37:07.128 --> 00:37:08.795
But you know
who has short nails?

769
00:37:08.896 --> 00:37:10.563
-I do.
-Me too.

770
00:37:10.663 --> 00:37:12.632
We all have short nails.
So what? We're all murderers?

771
00:37:12.732 --> 00:37:15.036
-What's your point. Morgan?
-This question is for Karadec.

772
00:37:16.103 --> 00:37:18.538
Do you know
who has short fingernails?

773
00:37:19.739 --> 00:37:22.609
Members of
the Long Beach Climbing Club.

774
00:37:24.979 --> 00:37:26.080
Kyle.

775
00:37:26.746 --> 00:37:28.883
-Explain.
-Kyle's a climber.

776
00:37:28.983 --> 00:37:30.717
[Morgan] <i>He could easily</i>
<i>get up in that window.</i>

777
00:37:30.818 --> 00:37:33.054
<i>He snuck up on Eduardo.</i>
<i>He forced him underwater.</i>

778
00:37:33.154 --> 00:37:34.889
Kyle wanted revenge
for his mother.

779
00:37:34.989 --> 00:37:37.825
Daphne. Oz.
go arrest Kyle Walker.
Get him back here ASAP.

780
00:37:40.161 --> 00:37:41.561
Excellent work. Morgan.

781
00:37:42.063 --> 00:37:44.664
Detective. get the confession
and close it up.

782
00:37:47.001 --> 00:37:48.169
I thought you quit.

783
00:37:48.269 --> 00:37:50.104
-Oh. I did.
-Hmm.

784
00:37:50.204 --> 00:37:51.272
But you need me. admit it.

785
00:37:51.972 --> 00:37:54.275
-Good work. Morgan.
-Thank you.

786
00:37:55.943 --> 00:37:57.577
You couldn't have done it alone.

787
00:37:57.677 --> 00:37:58.645
[radio chatter]

788
00:38:01.581 --> 00:38:02.582
What'd you say?

789
00:38:02.682 --> 00:38:04.952
I said. "You couldn't
have done it alone."

790
00:38:07.154 --> 00:38:10.124
Kyle. Kyle couldn't
have done it alone.

791
00:38:10.224 --> 00:38:12.126
He doesn't appear on
any of the CCTV footage.

792
00:38:12.226 --> 00:38:15.196
He had an accomplice.
someone who drugged
Eduardo's drink.

793
00:38:15.296 --> 00:38:16.596
[Morgan] <i>Someone who</i>
<i>unlatched the window.</i>

794
00:38:16.696 --> 00:38:19.266
Someone who was already
inside the room that night.

795
00:38:25.272 --> 00:38:28.275
I already explained
the nature of our relationship.

796
00:38:28.876 --> 00:38:34.115
Sure. But you left out the part
where you gave Eduardo $390.000.

797
00:38:34.215 --> 00:38:37.717
We know he lied to you.
We know he took your money.

798
00:38:38.319 --> 00:38:42.189
What we wanna know is.
how did you meet Kyle Walker?

799
00:38:50.831 --> 00:38:54.301
So. Kyle. how did you come into
contact with Dr. Iris Bowman?

800
00:38:57.805 --> 00:38:59.907
I met Kyle at the hotel.

801
00:39:00.374 --> 00:39:03.844
I started staking out
the place where I knew
she met this new guy.

802
00:39:03.944 --> 00:39:05.813
And that's where you met Iris?

803
00:39:05.913 --> 00:39:07.081
She was with him.

804
00:39:08.015 --> 00:39:09.682
I-- I just wanted
to ruin his scam.

805
00:39:09.782 --> 00:39:12.652
So. I tried to warn her.
but it was...

806
00:39:12.752 --> 00:39:16.323
Too late.
I-I'd already given him so much.

807
00:39:16.423 --> 00:39:22.229
I was humiliated. I was furious.
But Kyle wanted revenge.

808
00:39:22.329 --> 00:39:23.998
I'm saying that we get even.

809
00:39:24.098 --> 00:39:25.732
He came up
with a plan to kill him.

810
00:39:25.833 --> 00:39:29.837
It was Iris. She wanted revenge.

811
00:39:29.937 --> 00:39:31.272
The police can't help us.

812
00:39:31.372 --> 00:39:33.740
You could have
taken this to the authorities.

813
00:39:33.841 --> 00:39:34.909
We could have helped you.

814
00:39:35.009 --> 00:39:37.077
Help? No one could help me.

815
00:39:37.178 --> 00:39:39.346
One day I was married
to the love of my life.

816
00:39:39.446 --> 00:39:41.182
and the next day he was gone.

817
00:39:41.282 --> 00:39:47.021
I spent six months convinced
I'd never be whole again.
let alone love again.

818
00:39:47.121 --> 00:39:51.791
And then in a hotel bar.
of all places... [chuckles]
...I met Ernest.

819
00:39:51.892 --> 00:39:54.694
He brought light
back into my life.

820
00:39:56.130 --> 00:39:58.698
And in return.
I gave him everything.

821
00:39:59.366 --> 00:40:03.804
He said my money was
going to our vacation house
in Mexico City.

822
00:40:04.205 --> 00:40:07.707
So. once Kyle told me
about the scam. I just...

823
00:40:10.411 --> 00:40:12.279
decided to take back
what was mine.

824
00:40:13.881 --> 00:40:16.383
See. that's the thing. Kyle.
It doesn't matter
who came up with what.

825
00:40:16.483 --> 00:40:17.418
You're both complicit.

826
00:40:17.717 --> 00:40:19.786
Can I ask you guys something?

827
00:40:20.888 --> 00:40:26.726
What kind of man takes advantage
of a woman whose memory
is-is so far gone

828
00:40:26.827 --> 00:40:29.230
that she doesn't even know
who or where she is?

829
00:40:30.898 --> 00:40:34.734
Who targets a mother
whose memory is so eroded

830
00:40:34.835 --> 00:40:36.837
that she doesn't even
remember her son?

831
00:40:38.738 --> 00:40:40.441
Come on. Kyle. It's time to go.

832
00:40:41.242 --> 00:40:42.910
Hey. wait. Wait. wait. wait.

833
00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:46.280
Before you lock me up
or whatever you're gonna do...

834
00:40:48.449 --> 00:40:50.918
I need to say goodbye to my mom.

835
00:41:03.998 --> 00:41:04.999
All set.

836
00:41:06.033 --> 00:41:08.768
<i>Hey. Mom. I'm in Colorado now.</i>

837
00:41:08.869 --> 00:41:10.938
You got the job.

838
00:41:11.038 --> 00:41:14.208
<i>Yeah. you're looking at</i>
<i>Rocky Mountain Park's</i>
<i>newest employee.</i>

839
00:41:14.308 --> 00:41:19.280
Oh. that is fantastic. sweetie.
You always wanted to move there.

840
00:41:20.447 --> 00:41:22.516
I'm not gonna be able
to stop by anymore.

841
00:41:23.217 --> 00:41:26.786
But I-I'm gonna call you. okay?
I'm gonna check in.

842
00:41:28.122 --> 00:41:30.291
Will you promise me
that you're gonna keep up
with your physical--

843
00:41:30.391 --> 00:41:33.294
<i>You worry too much about me.</i>
<i>You always have.</i>

844
00:41:33.394 --> 00:41:34.895
I'll be okay.

845
00:41:35.296 --> 00:41:36.497
<i>Hey. I love you. Mom.</i>

846
00:41:37.797 --> 00:41:39.967
Oh. I love you too.

847
00:41:50.411 --> 00:41:53.347
Karadec. I was wrong.

848
00:41:53.981 --> 00:41:56.550
-You're not
a heartless police robot.
-Mmm.

849
00:41:56.817 --> 00:41:59.019
Just a regular police robot.

850
00:42:00.387 --> 00:42:02.990
Well. I'll. uh.
see you tomorrow.

851
00:42:13.167 --> 00:42:14.168
[chuckles]

852
00:42:23.510 --> 00:42:24.511
Mmm.

852
00:42:25.305 --> 00:43:25.393
